You can raise errors of your own. Declare variables in a `DECLARE` section before `BEGIN`, assign with `:=`, and test with `IF`:

```sql harness
CREATE FUNCTION withdraw(who text) RETURNS text
    LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
{{code}}
    RETURN 'ok';
EXCEPTION
    WHEN OTHERS THEN
        RETURN SQLERRM;
END
$$;

SELECT withdraw('Ana') AS ana, withdraw('Cleo') AS cleo;
```

```sql
DECLARE
    new_balance numeric;
BEGIN
    new_balance := (SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE owner = who);
    IF new_balance < 10 THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'not enough money';
    END IF;
```

`RAISE EXCEPTION 'message'` fails just like a built-in error. Uncaught, it stops the program:

```text
ERROR:  not enough money
```

`WHEN OTHERS` catches any error, and inside the handler `SQLERRM` holds its message, so `RETURN SQLERRM;` reports what went wrong instead of failing.

The key rule: **an EXCEPTION block undoes its own changes.** PostgreSQL sets a savepoint when the block starts, and when a handler catches an error it rolls back to that savepoint, so every row the block changed is put back. Variables keep their values. So you can update first and check after.

One gotcha: don't give a variable or parameter a column's name (like `balance`). PostgreSQL stops with `column reference "balance" is ambiguous`.

example.code:
CREATE FUNCTION withdraw(who text, amount numeric) RETURNS text
    LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
DECLARE
    new_balance numeric;
BEGIN
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - amount WHERE owner = who;
    new_balance := (SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE owner = who);
    IF new_balance < 10 THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'not enough money';
    END IF;
    RETURN 'ok';
EXCEPTION
    WHEN OTHERS THEN
        RETURN SQLERRM;
END
$$;

SELECT withdraw('Ana', 50) AS ana, withdraw('Ben', 40) AS ben;

SELECT * FROM accounts ORDER BY id;

